digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56295-x
  rankdir="TB";
  "I1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: Climate is worsening, and research targets capture, so CO2 capture is a critical need."];
  "I10" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: Porosity enhancement is key, and precise strategy is needed, so precise porosity engineering is required."];
  "I12" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: Large ligands are not applicable, and they lower decomp temp, so ligand-directed strategy is limited."];
  "I2" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: MOFs have porous structures, and porous structures enable capture, so MOFs are suitable for capture."];
  "I23" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: Glasses are viscoelastic, and methods exist, so bulk fabrication is feasible."];
  "I3" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: CO2 capture is needed, and MOFs are suitable, so MOFs are a candidate solution."];
  "I4" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: MOFs need processing, but powders are unstable, so a new processing form is hypothesized."];
  "I5" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: MOFs are candidates, but need new form, so processable MOFs are required."];
  "I8" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: Porosity is reduced in glass, but enhancement is imperative, so porosity enhancement is the key challenge."];
  "I9" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: Agents can enhance porosity, but precise manipulation is challenging, so a precise strategy is needed."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes and evaluates a strategy in which bulky amide solvents act as structure-directing agents with mixed imidazole/benzimidazole ligands to synthesize meltable 12-membered-ring ZIF frameworks, melt-quench them into highly porous, recrystallization-resistant MOF glasses with Å-scale porosity, and test their gas adsorption plus CH4/N2 and CO2/N2 separation performance.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A coherent MOF-glass research program links the need for processable porous frameworks, structure-directed 12-membered-ring ZIF synthesis, recrystallization suppression, melt-quench glass formation, retained Å-scale porosity, adsorption, and membrane gas-separation evaluation."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) For meltable and porous MOFs, topology engineering using nodes and ligands similar to meltable MOFs must account for chemical composition because composition governs melting and decomposition behavior."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Because large or extra-ligand strategies are limited for meltable MOFs, a porosity strategy that does not rely on introducing large extra ligands is motivated."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The obtained ZIF set includes CAN and AFI topologies with 12-membered rings, and AFI-[Zn(Im)2] specifically displays a large 15.6 Å pore aperture."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) For CAN/AFI-[Zn(Im)2], recrystallization to dense ZIF-zni before melting reduces porosity in the resultant melt-quenched glass, motivating recrystallization suppression for these SDA-derived frameworks."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Combining bulky amide SDAs with mixed Im/bIm ligands is motivated as a design strategy to pair SDA-generated porous topology with ligand-supported suppression of recrystallization."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) The AFI/CAN-[Zn(Im)(bIm)] frameworks show greater porosity than ZIF-62, and the reported carry-over of enhanced porosity into glass with high adsorption capacity supports the strategy as capable of producing porous MOF glass in successful cases."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) A free-standing membrane with record-breaking separation performance supports, but does not fully validate, promising gas-purification and CO2-capture applications for porous MOF glass, including CH4/N2 and CO2/N2 separation contexts."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence for porous glass formation with high adsorption capacity together with membrane separation performance supports the strategy as promising, not conclusively validated."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) If ZIF-derived MOF glasses are pursued as processable MOF candidates, then precise porosity engineering motivates porous ZIF/MOF glasses as a research target."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) The need for porous processable ZIF/MOF glasses, together with limits of large extra-ligand strategies, motivates a specific alternative porosity-engineering strategy."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) Prior use of large solvent molecules as SDAs, including DBF and DPF in Shi et al.'s large-pore ZIF synthesis, supports bulky solvent SDAs as a viable route to porous ZIF frameworks without extra ligands."];
  "R30" [label="(0,0,0) SDA-derived porosity is promising, but for melt-quenchable glass precursors it needs stabilization against recrystallization that would reduce glass porosity."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Some ZIFs are MOFs that can form glasses via melt-quenching, supporting the existence of ZIF-derived MOF glasses without claiming that all ZIFs form glasses."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because MOF glasses are processable and some ZIF-derived MOF glasses exist, ZIF-derived MOF glasses are a plausible processable MOF-glass form."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Given the need for processable MOFs, ZIF-derived MOF glasses are a candidate target rather than the uniquely required target."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Phenomenon: Excessive energy consumption and fossil fuels worsen climate change."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Case: Melt-quenched MOF glasses display viscoelastic states above Tg."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Knowledge: Forming methods like hot-pressing explored to fabricate bulk samples."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Phenomenon: Upon glass formation, inherent porosity in crystalline precursors is reduced."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Knowledge: Substantial enhancement in porosity is imperative for MOF glasses for gas separation."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Case: Introduction of pore-inducing agents can enhance porosity through decomposition."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) Phenomenon: Achieving precise manipulation of porosity at the Ångstrom scale remains a challenge."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Rule: Chemical composition (nodes/ligands) determines melting point and decomposition temperature."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Case: To make meltable and porous MOFs, it is logical to engineer topology using similar nodes/ligands to meltable MOFs."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Rule: Ligand-directed strategy using large ligands is not readily applicable to meltable MOFs."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Case: Introduction of extra ligands may potentially lower the decomposition temperature."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Knowledge: Extensive research focuses on carbon capture to reduce greenhouse gases."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) Common: Large solvent molecules have been used as SDAs to synthesize porous MOFs without extra ligands."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Case: Shi et al. synthesized large pore ZIFs using DBF and DPF solvents as SDAs."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Case: Obtained ZIFs possess CAN and AFI topology with 12-membered rings."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Case: AFI-[Zn(Im)2] displays pore apertures as large as 15.6 Å."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) Phenomenon: CAN/AFI-[Zn(Im)2] will recrystallize to dense ZIF-zni phase before melting."];
  "S25" [label="(25,0,0) Knowledge: This leads to a reduction in the porosity of the resultant melt-quenched glass."];
  "S27" [label="(27,0,0) Rule: Bulky amide solvents (SDAs) create highly porous topologies without introducing extra ligands."];
  "S28" [label="(28,0,0) Case: Mixed ligands of Im and bIm suppress recrystallization and provide spatial support."];
  "S29" [label="(29,0,0) Case: Obtained frameworks AFI/CAN-[Zn(Im)(bIm)] possess similar composition to ZIF-62 but greater porosity."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Case: MOFs are cage-like structures with metal nodes and organic ligands."];
  "S30" [label="(30,0,0) Case: Enhanced porosity carries over to glass, resulting in high adsorption capacities."];
  "S31" [label="(31,0,0) Case: Free-standing membrane fabricated with record-breaking separation performance."];
  "S32" [label="(32,0,0) Common: Porous MOF glass shows promising applications in energy gas purification and CO2 capture."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Rule: Highly porous MOF structures enable applications in gas separations like CO2 capture."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Case: Crystalline MOFs require processing like pelletization for device applications."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Phenomenon: Practical implementation is hampered by mechanical stability limitations of powders."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Rule: MOF glasses open opportunities to process MOFs into macroscale, grain-boundary free morphologies."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Case: ZIFs are a class of MOFs containing tetrahedral metal ions linked by imidazolate ions."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) Case: A few types of ZIFs have been discovered with the capability to form glasses via melt-quenching."];
  "S1" -> "I1" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
